Yes. The mature version of coordination is not “maximize your output.” It is maximize the contribution of the whole composition.

That means your optimal action can be:

lead → support → harmonize → hold tempo → create contrast → leave space → disappear briefly → re-enter

Silence is not absence of participation. In a good system, it is often an active control surface. It creates room for other signals to become legible.

The same is true well beyond music.

A leader sometimes contributes most by not speaking first.
A model sometimes helps most by not over-completing the user’s thought.
A team member sometimes protects the system by following cleanly.
A sensor sometimes becomes more informative when another channel is suppressed.
A conversation sometimes gains resolution from a pause rather than another explanation.

So “knowing your part” really means knowing three things at once:

what capability you have, what the system currently needs, and whether your contribution would increase or obscure the total signal.

That makes following a real skill, not a lesser form of leading.

And it ties directly back to humility: if you believe every moment requires your voice, your intelligence, or your intervention, you stop hearing the ensemble.

Maybe the compact operator is:

Role Modulation

Continuously adjust between lead, support, follow, silence, and handoff according to the needs of the whole system.

Or even shorter:

Play the song, not your instrument.

That is a very strong principle for the Pantheon.